Sleeping in a Loire Valley château means swapping the standardised hotel room for a space that has a name, a history and grounds for its only neighbour. For years, at the Château de la Huberdière near Amboise, we have welcomed travellers who came looking for an exceptional night rather than a simple stopover bed.

Here are seven ways to live it, from a room with character to the exclusive hire of the whole estate.

## What options are there for sleeping in a Loire Valley château?

You can sleep in a Loire Valley château either in a room within a charming hotel or by hiring a whole estate to yourself. The valley holds a density of them found nowhere else in France, between Orléans and Angers, and the Amboise area is its heart: Chenonceau, Chaumont-sur-Loire and the royal château all sit within half an hour's drive.

The Huberdière stands in Nazelles-Négron, just across the Loire from Amboise. You are 2 hrs 20 from Paris, 30 minutes from Tours and its Saint-Pierre-des-Corps TGV station, and already far from the bustle. The estate is a 16th-century family home: 10 rooms and suites, walled grounds of fourteen hectares, and the kind of silence you thought you had forgotten. The rooms and rates are set out on the [château stay](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise) page.

## Choosing a room with a name, not a number

The first way to live this night is to choose a room rather than a standardised room category. Our 10 rooms each carry a name and a decor that belongs to it alone: Paradis Sauvage, Giboulée, Pêche, Bleu Indien. Parquet that creaks a little, high ceilings, a window onto the centuries-old trees or the Vouvray hillsides.

![Bedroom lined with patterned green wallpaper, double bed and bedside lamp](/images/inline/chambres/chambre-07.webp "Each room has a name and its own wallpaper. No two look alike.")

No two are alike, and that is deliberate. Regulars have their favourite and ask for it again from one year to the next. You are not booking a door number, you are choosing an atmosphere. To understand what one really looks for in a charming address in the Touraine, our guide on [bed and breakfast in Amboise](/en/blog/bed-and-breakfast-amboise) tells what sets a lived-in home apart from a place to pass through.

## Waking with the windows open onto the grounds

The second pleasure comes at first light, when you push the shutters open onto fourteen walled hectares and hear nothing but the birds. No road, no car horn: the grounds are ringed with walls, and the calm is almost physical.

You go down for coffee on the terrace, the grass still damp with dew, the children off barefoot without a gate to watch. This is the silence you come looking for in a château rather than a town centre, and it keeps its promise: the night is truly dark here, and you wake to the rhythm of the Touraine countryside, not to car horns.

## Dining at the host's table without getting back in the car

The third pleasure, and no small one, is dining on site, a glass in hand, without hunting for a restaurant or getting back in the car. I am Italian: on Thursday evenings the kitchen smells of garlic and basil from five o'clock. That evening dinner is entirely Italian, fresh pasta included, and no one leaves the table without a second helping.

On the other evenings, except Sunday, there are large sharing boards around a single table, simpler, but the rule does not change: we dine together, without getting back in the car. This home cooking, made with produce from the market and the kitchen garden, is as much a part of the stay as the room. The evenings we serve are set out on the [dining at the château](/en/chateau-dining-touraine) page.

## A swim in the heated pool at sunset

The fourth pleasure belongs to the warm season: a heated pool, open from April to October, set in the middle of the grounds, away from prying eyes. You swim late, as the light drops over the hillsides and the heat of the day fades.

![The long heated pool, framed by trees and stone paving](/images/inline/piscine/piscine-03.webp "The pool is heated and open from April to October, tucked out of sight in the walled grounds.")

It is the kind of detail that turns a night into a stay. You come back from a day at Chenonceau, you leave your things in the room, and you dive in before dinner rather than chasing a terrace in town. The water stays warm until September, when the vines begin to turn yellow all around.

## Having the château to yourself, with friends or family

The fifth pleasure is having the whole house to yourselves. The Huberdière can be hired exclusively for a night or a weekend, with its 10 rooms, its 21 beds and its fourteen hectares reserved for your group alone. No other guests in the sitting rooms, no imposed schedule: you are at home.

It is the format for family reunions, milestone birthdays and weekends with friends, with the table looked after by the house and the room to be truly together, late into the evening under the lime trees of the park. We set out this format in our guide on [hiring a château with friends or family](/en/blog/rent-chateau-group-weekend), and the [family](/en/chateau-hire-large-group) page brings together the estate's capacities and options.

## Making the château your base camp for the valley

The sixth pleasure is roaming without ever changing hotel. From the Huberdière, the finest monuments in the valley are within easy reach by car: you set off to visit in the morning, you come home to your grounds in the evening, you dine at the house, and you start again the next day without repacking.

A few landmarks from the estate:

- the royal château of Amboise and the Clos Lucé, Leonardo da Vinci's last home, ten minutes away
- Chenonceau, the château set over the Cher, twenty minutes away
- Chaumont-sur-Loire and its international garden festival, twenty-five minutes away
- the Vouvray and Montlouis cellars for tastings
- the Loire à Vélo cycle route, along the banks of the Loire

To build your itinerary, our guide on [visiting the Loire châteaux](/en/blog/visiting-loire-chateaux) gives the distances, opening times and visiting order that avoids the crowds.

## Coming off-season, the estate at its finest

The seventh pleasure is the one people often forget: coming outside the summer. Autumn reddens the Vouvray vines at harvest time, winter has the charm of absolute calm and a fire in the hearth, spring wakes the gardens of Villandry and Chaumont in soft light and almost no crowds.

![The estate's trees in autumn colour, in front of a stone wall](/images/inline/chateau-exterieur/chateau-exterieur-10.webp "October and November are the quietest months on the estate, and probably the finest.")

There is no bad season to sleep in a Loire Valley château, only different moods. An off-season weekend for two often gives the estate at its finest: the empty paths, a sitting room to yourself, and the silence that summer has to share with everyone.

## What makes the price of a night vary?

A night in a charming château usually ranges from 150 to 300 euros per room, home-made breakfast included. The price depends on the room chosen, the season and the length of the stay. Rather than a single rate, we prefer to show the real availability and let you choose the room that speaks to you, on the [château stay](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise) page.

For a group, the logic changes: you think in terms of exclusive hire of the estate, per night, according to the number of beds used. At a comparable budget, a château offers what an ordinary hotel does not: the space of grounds, a home table and the feeling of being received rather than lodged.

## Guesthouse or château hotel: what is the difference?

The real choice is not between two labels, but between two atmospheres that often meet. You may want the welcome of a bed and breakfast, that closeness to the people who run the place, and at the same time the comfort and services of a hotel. That is exactly the spirit in which we welcome guests: a family home that has kept the welcome of a bed and breakfast, with the comfort of a charming hotel.

What you gain by sleeping in a château like ours is coherence: the room, the grounds, the table and the history of the place form a whole. You do not stack up a hotel, a restaurant and a visit, you live the estate for a night.

For a night, a weekend or an exclusive hire among friends, write to us and we will prepare your stay. You receive a proposal within 24 hours, with the available rooms and our seasonal advice.
